  {
    "text": "Partial observability necessitates that an agent uses historical observations to resolve state ambiguity, thereby requiring a translation from memory to action selection, even without an explicit overwritable world model.",
    "topic": "Memory-to-plan under partial observability"
  },
  {
    "text": "An explicit world model that gets overwritten introduces a distinct challenge because the agent must reconcile potentially conflicting information between its persistent long-term memory and the newly updated model.",
    "topic": "Overwriting world model introduces additional complexity"
  },
  {
    "text": "In embodied agents, long-term memory often encodes stable knowledge while the world model tracks dynamic, stateful environment changes; overwriting the latter can disrupt plan coherence derived from the former.",
    "topic": "Distinct roles of memory and world model"
  },
  {
    "text": "Empirical studies on partially observable planning show that agents relying solely on recurrent memory without an overwritable world model still face the translation challenge, indicating partial observability alone is sufficient.",
    "topic": "Sufficiency of partial observability"
  }
]
